I worked on integrating LangChain OpenWiki with a Python codebase to automatically generate and maintain code documentation.
The underlying project is a Python financial library based on numpy-financial.
The library provides financial functions such as:
•	fv() → Future Value
•	pv() → Present Value
•	pmt() → Payment
•	irr() → Internal Rate of Return
•	For example, IRR calculates the Internal Rate of Return for a series of cash flows.

OpenWiki is an AI-powered codebase documentation tool.
I installed OpenWiki globally:
npm install -g openwiki
Then verified the installation:
openwiki --help
CONFIGURING GEMINI
Configure the AI provider
Gemini AI Studio.
A code wiki for this repository.
So I configured OpenWiki using:
OPENWIKI_PROVIDER="gemini"
OPENWIKI_MODEL_ID="gemini-3.1-flash-lite"
GEMINI_API_KEY="YOUR_GEMINI_API_KEY"
Openwiki –init 
openwiki visualize

The configuration was stored in:
~/.openwiki/.env
There is also an:
AGENTS.md
which helps coding agents understand the generated documentation.

When OpenWiki analyzes the repository, it creates documentation inside:
1. financial-functions.md This is the human-readable documentation.
•	What the financial functions do
•	How functions are related
•	Important implementation details
•	Dependencies and code flow
schemaVersion defines the structure of the metadata, while pageVersion tracks the revision of an individual generated documentation page
2. .claims/
This contains OpenWiki's claims/evidence information.
Think of it as:
“What does the documentation claim, and what source code supports that claim?”
So OpenWiki can maintain documentation based on the actual repository instead of treating the Markdown as completely independent documentation.
3. .page-manifest.json
This is basically OpenWiki's page tracking/index information.
It keeps information about the generated documentation pages, such as their identity/version-related metadata.
You may see fields like:
{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "pageVersion": 2
}
schemaVersion → tells OpenWiki which structure/version of the metadata format is being used.
pageVersion → tracks the version/revision of that particular generated page.
So you can explain:
“The manifest helps OpenWiki keep track of its generated documentation pages and their metadata versions.”
________________________________________
4. .last-update.json
This stores information about the last OpenWiki update.
It helps OpenWiki understand the previous documentation state and determine what needs to be updated when the code changes.

MAKE A CODE CHANGE
git status
git diff
git add .
git commit -m "feat: update financial calculation"
Push to GitHub
git push origin main
Now the source-code change exists on GitHub.
OPENWIKI UPDATE
Run OpenWiki update
This is different from --init.
For an existing OpenWiki documentation set, I use:
openwiki –update
Commit OpenWiki changes
After verifying the generated documentation:
git add openwiki
Then:
git commit -m "docs: update OpenWiki documentation"
Then:
git push origin main

